# Android Architecture Rules

## Architecture

Use MVVM with Clean Architecture principles.

Recommended logical layers:

- Presentation
- Domain
- Data
- Core

## Dependency Direction

- Presentation depends on Domain.
- Data implements Domain repository interfaces.
- Domain must not depend on Android framework, Retrofit, or Compose.
- Core contains shared infrastructure and reusable components.

Do not allow dependencies to flow in the wrong direction.

## Presentation Layer

- Use Jetpack Compose for UI.
- Use ViewModels to manage screen state and UI-related logic.
- Expose screen state using StateFlow.
- Use SharedFlow or Channel for one-time UI events when appropriate.
- Collect flows using lifecycle-aware collection.
- Do not call Retrofit APIs directly from Composables or ViewModels.
- Keep Composables focused on rendering and user interactions.

## UI State Organization

- Define screen UI states using dedicated immutable data classes.
- Keep primary UI state data classes in separate files from their corresponding ViewModels.
- Organize UI state files within their respective feature and screen packages.
- Prefer a single primary UiState data class per screen to represent its complete UI state.
- Keep UI state focused on presentation requirements.
- Define separate UI event classes when one-time events require explicit modeling.
- Extract shared state models only when multiple screens genuinely share the same responsibility.
- Avoid creating unnecessary state classes for simple local or temporary UI values.
- Do not place unrelated screen states in a single shared state file.

## UI State Management

- Model UI state based on meaningful screen-specific conditions.
- Avoid relying exclusively on generic Success and Error states when the screen requires more descriptive states.
- Use immutable data classes or sealed interfaces/classes depending on the complexity of the screen.
- State names must clearly communicate the UI condition they represent.
- Keep screen data and related UI state together when using data class-based state management.
- Support loading, content, empty, and error states where applicable.
- Preserve existing content during refresh operations when appropriate.
- Keep UI state transitions predictable and explicit.
- Use StateFlow to expose the current screen state.
- Use SharedFlow or Channel for one-time UI events, not persistent screen state.

## Domain Layer

The Domain layer must contain business models, repository interfaces, and use cases.

### UseCase Pattern

- UseCases must represent distinct business operations.
- Each UseCase must have a single, clearly defined responsibility.
- UseCases must depend on repository interfaces, never on concrete Data layer implementations.
- ViewModels must invoke UseCases instead of directly accessing repositories.
- UseCases must use constructor injection.
- UseCases should expose a single `operator fun invoke()` when appropriate.
- Use suspend functions for one-shot asynchronous operations and Flow for stream-based operations.
- Keep business logic inside the Domain layer, not in ViewModels.
- Avoid unnecessary UseCases that only introduce boilerplate without meaningful separation of responsibility.

### Repository Contracts

- Define repository interfaces in the Domain layer.
- Repository interfaces must expose domain models, not API DTOs.
- Data layer implementations must fulfill these contracts.

### Domain Independence

- Domain must not depend on Android framework, Jetpack Compose, Retrofit, or infrastructure-specific implementations.

## Data Layer

- Implement repository interfaces from the Domain layer.
- Keep API DTOs separate from domain models.
- Use mappers to convert DTOs into domain models.
- Handle network and data errors explicitly.
- Keep Retrofit and OkHttp implementation details inside the Data/Core infrastructure.

## Data Layer - API Response and Error Handling

- Keep API response processing separate from Repository implementations.
- Use dedicated reusable components to handle HTTP responses and API errors.
- Centralize API error parsing and exception normalization.
- Handle network connectivity errors, HTTP errors, parsing errors, and unexpected exceptions consistently.
- Repository implementations must coordinate API calls, DTO mapping, and domain-level results.
- Avoid duplicating try-catch blocks and HTTP error parsing logic across repositories.
- Do not expose Retrofit Response objects, API DTOs, or raw HTTP exceptions to the Domain or Presentation layers.
- Use a consistent error representation at the repository boundary.
- Preserve meaningful server error messages when available.
- Do not silently convert failed API requests into successful empty responses.

## Coroutines and Flow

- Use suspend functions for one-shot asynchronous operations.
- Use Flow for asynchronous streams.
- Use StateFlow for observable UI state.
- Use SharedFlow or Channel for one-time events when appropriate.
- Never block the main thread.
- Handle coroutine cancellation correctly.
